          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">Step 1: Define Your Particle Size</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            What size particle do you need to capture? Use a particle size analyzer or reference material specifications. If capturing 100-micron particles, choose a mesh with 100-micron or smaller rating.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">Step 2: Choose Filtration Efficiency</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm mb-2">
            <strong>Nominal filtration:</strong> Captures ~98% of particles at rated size. Standard for most applications.
          </p>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            <strong>Absolute filtration:</strong> Captures 99.9%+ of particles. Required for critical applications (pharma, electronics). Use dutch weave for absolute ratings below 100 microns.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">Step 3: Balance Flow Rate and Retention</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            Finer mesh = lower flow rate. If your process requires high throughput, consider:
          </p>
