    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">7. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What is the difference between mesh count and micron rating?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Mesh count measures wires per inch; micron rating measures particle size captured. A 100-mesh screen typically filters 150-micron particles. The relationship depends on wire diameter—thicker wire means smaller openings at the same mesh count.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Why does the same mesh count have different micron ratings?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Wire diameter varies. A 100-mesh screen with 0.10mm wire filters ~150 microns. With 0.08mm wire, it filters ~154 microns. Always specify both mesh count and wire diameter when ordering.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What is the finest mesh size available?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Standard woven mesh goes up to 635 mesh (~20 microns). For finer filtration, use dutch weave (down to 1 micron) or consider non-woven alternatives like sintered powder or membrane filters.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How do I measure mesh count on an existing sample?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Use a mesh counter or magnifying glass with scale. Count wires in one linear inch. For accuracy, count in 3 different locations and average. A ruler works for coarse mesh (under 50); use a loupe or microscope for fine mesh.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What is the difference between SWG and AWG wire gauge?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            SWG (Standard Wire Gauge) is British; AWG (American Wire Gauge) is American. Same gauge number = different diameters. For example, 20 SWG = 0.91mm; 20 AWG = 0.81mm. Always specify wire diameter in millimeters to avoid confusion.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

